plan.angle:
ONE PLAN FROM THREE SPECS.
The judges split. One chose A (calm and discoverable), narrowly ahead of B. The other chose C (robustness and e-ink engineering). Their lists of what to borrow still agree on the outcome, so this plan combines them:
- From A: the screens, the experience and the twin rule. Every gesture has a visible, labelled button that does the same thing; the page stays home; you see a preview before anything commits; each moment is one Undo step; and the Shapes button is never shown inverted.
- From B: how the Links bar commits and cancels (tap the page to stay; the bar's own close goes back). Also its entries are Index entries, the eye is literally dimmed when off, Link to... offers Recent targets and moves to the top of the screen when the drawn area sits low, link targets are prefetched so jumps are instant, there is a back list, and the Index button opens a view titled 'Index' with Graph and List.
- From C: the engineering order and the safety rules. Measure the panel before freezing thresholds. One pure touch recognizer is fed by the Kindle and the simulator alike. Scenarios switch to symbolic panel taps before the panel grows. Areas go on separate area lines, so the device's f9d13f5 build still reads every link line. The lasso is a UI overlay and never ink. The flash every 30th refresh stays. The pen-near rule loosens in stages.

Every flaw the judges found is fixed where it occurs:
- the bar committing on finger down, when the first finger of a two-finger swipe begins with that same touch;
- the double pinch dead zone;
- legacy pinch values the new contract would have broken;
- overscroll counted per direction instead of per axis;
- an inverted Shapes button on a panel where inverted means 'current tool';
- area migration that save_page never writes, because save_page saves links.ini only when the page itself is dirty (verified at editor.c:751);
- page reads on the UI thread with no pump (store_read_page has no work_pump; only page_render pumps);
- a link undo that loses its step when the save fails (history_undo has already moved it to redo, history.c:177);
- Copy page losing area links;
- P_NEXT adding a page at the end when the move came from a gesture (editor.c:1862);
- the area area-weighted refresh rule, dropped because writing's settles count toward the flash rule (app.c:931).

SIX RULES YOU CAN FEEL
1. The page is home. Making, following, renaming, retargeting and deleting a link all happen over the page. Only the Pages view and the Index map fill the screen, and both open only when you ask.
2. Two fingers navigate, one finger taps and holds, the pen writes. A gesture fires when the fingers lift, never under a resting palm or a hovering pen.
3. Every gesture has a visible twin:
   - two fingers down = the page chip;
   - two fingers up = the Links button;
   - left and right = the arrows;
   - Go to, New link, Areas and Index are labelled buttons in the bar;
   - Smart shapes = a panel button plus a labelled row in the tool picker.
4. Look, then commit. A jump from the bar is a peek: tap the page outside the bar to stay (one GC16), or use the bar's own close to go back. A shape snap is a preview until the pen lifts.
5. Undo covers everything. Making, deleting, retargeting or redrawing a link is one step, and so is each snapped shape.
6. Your words are the defaults. Where the plan departs from them it says so, and the departures that change the build are among the 8 questions.

WHERE THIS DEPARTS FROM THE BRIEF'S LITERAL WORDS
(a) Sideways or zoomed in, a two-finger drag in mid-screen still scrolls the page, and the navigation swipes start at an edge band instead. Upright at the fitted view, 'two fingers down' works anywhere, as you wrote. (Question 4.)
(b) 'Record a gesture' means choosing from a fixed set Grimoire can tell apart reliably, not drawing a free-form shape. (Question 6.)
(c) The long-press card also offers 'Change where it goes' and 'Redraw the area'. Areas are invisible, so Redraw is the only way to fix one without deleting the link. It drops 'Go there', because a tap already goes there.
(d) A pen touch outside the bar stays on the shown page, but that stroke is not drawn. A closing refresh still on the panel would take the stroke off fast pen mode.
(e) Rename changes the target page's name in the Index, everywhere it appears. (Question 3.)
(f) The built-in gestures add page turns on two fingers left and right. (Question 5.)
(g) Three things cannot be assigned: language, calibration and the Enable shortcuts switch, because a binding that turns off its own source is a trap. The gestures on/off switch can be assigned only to keys and controller buttons.

